window.QBANK=(window.QBANK||[]).concat([{"id":"ecg-01","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Action potentials & pacemaking","difficulty":1,"stem":"An investigator records transmembrane potentials from a single cell isolated from the heart. The cell has no stable resting potential. After each action potential the membrane reaches its most negative value (about −60 mV) and then slowly depolarizes until the next action potential fires. The upstroke is slow, persists after the fast Na⁺ channel blocker tetrodotoxin, and is abolished by a Ca²⁺ channel blocker. There is no plateau.\n\nWhich of the following statements about this cell is most accurate?","figure":null,"choices":["Its slow diastolic depolarization begins with an inward Na⁺ “funny” current (If) through HCN channels that are activated by hyperpolarization at the end of phase 3","Its membrane potential between beats is held near the K⁺ equilibrium potential by a large inward-rectifier K⁺ current (IK1)","It is most likely a Purkinje fiber, the fastest-conducting tissue in the heart","Its upstroke is produced by voltage-gated Na⁺ channels that open when the membrane crosses about −65 mV","It conducts impulses to neighboring cells at about 4 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is a slow-response (pacemaker) action potential, as seen in SA and AV nodal cells: unstable phase 4, a Ca²⁺-dependent phase 0, and no real plateau. The lecture's phase 4 sequence is: the hyperpolarization at the end of phase 3 activates If (inward Na⁺ through HCN channels), T-type Ca²⁺ channels then move the membrane toward the L-type threshold (about −40 mV), and outward K⁺ current fades over the same period. Slow-response cells lack the stabilizing IK1 current, so they have no flat resting potential near EK. Nodal conduction is slow (about 0.05 m/s).","whyWrong":["","A large IK1 is what keeps fast-response atrial and ventricular cells flat at about −90 mV in phase 4. Slow-response nodal cells lack IK1, which is why their phase 4 drifts upward.","Purkinje fibers are fast-response cells with a Na⁺-driven upstroke, a plateau, and a stable phase 4 at baseline. Tetrodotoxin would block their upstroke.","Opening of voltage-gated Na⁺ channels at about −65 mV is phase 0 of the fast-response AP. This upstroke survives tetrodotoxin and is blocked by a Ca²⁺ channel blocker, so it is carried by Ca²⁺.","About 4 m/s is Purkinje conduction velocity. Slow-response nodal tissue conducts at about 0.05 m/s."],"objective":"Distinguish slow-response (nodal) from fast-response action potentials by the currents that drive phase 0 and phase 4.","source":"Electrophysiology I (Beaser): slow-response AP and automaticity"},{"id":"ecg-02","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Action potentials & pacemaking","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has palpitations. Her ECG shows sinus tachycardia at 120/min. During carotid sinus massage her heart rate gradually falls to 90/min, and the P waves in lead II stay upright throughout.\n\nWhich change in her sinoatrial (SA) nodal cells most directly accounts for the slowing?","figure":null,"choices":["Norepinephrine acting on β1 receptors increases If, steepening phase 4 and lowering the firing threshold","Acetylcholine acting on M2 receptors lowers cAMP (reducing If) and opens K⁺ channels, which makes the maximum diastolic potential more negative and flattens phase 4","Acetylcholine acting on nicotinic receptors on nodal cells depolarizes them and inactivates their Ca²⁺ channels","Acetylcholine blocks fast Na⁺ channels in nodal cells, slowing the phase 0 upstroke","Increased L-type Ca²⁺ current lengthens the nodal plateau and delays the next action potential"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Carotid sinus massage stretches the carotid baroreceptors. Baroreceptor firing rises, and the medulla responds by increasing vagal outflow and reducing sympathetic outflow. Postganglionic vagal fibers release acetylcholine onto muscarinic M2 receptors on SA nodal cells. Gi signaling lowers cAMP, so less If is activated, and K⁺ conductance rises. The maximum diastolic potential becomes more negative and the phase 4 slope becomes shallower, so threshold is reached later and the sinus rate falls. Sinus origin is unchanged (P waves stay upright). Sympathetic β1 stimulation does the opposite: the lecture describes it as increasing If and lowering the firing threshold.","whyWrong":["This describes sympathetic β1 stimulation, which speeds the sinus rate. Carotid massage increases vagal tone and reduces sympathetic tone.","","Nicotinic receptors are at autonomic ganglia, not on nodal cells. The vagal effect on the SA node goes through muscarinic (M2) receptors, and it hyperpolarizes the cell rather than depolarizing it.","SA nodal phase 0 does not depend on fast Na⁺ channels; it is Ca²⁺-mediated. Acetylcholine slows the rate mainly by acting on phase 4.","Nodal cells have essentially no plateau. More L-type Ca²⁺ current would speed depolarization, not slow it, and vagal stimulation reduces Ca²⁺ current rather than increasing it."],"objective":"Explain how vagal (M2) and sympathetic (β1) input change SA nodal phase 4 slope and maximum diastolic potential to change heart rate.","source":"Electrophysiology I & III (Beaser); Autonomic and Extrinsic Regulation"},{"id":"ecg-03","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Action potentials & pacemaking","difficulty":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with recurrent palpitations starts verapamil, a non-dihydropyridine L-type Ca²⁺ channel blocker that acts on the heart. Before treatment his ECG showed sinus rhythm at 72/min, PR 160 ms, and QRS 90 ms. His lead II rhythm strip two weeks later is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best explains why the PR interval lengthened while the QRS duration did not change?","figure":null,"choices":["The drug slowed conduction through the His–Purkinje system, which normally conducts at about 4 m/s","The drug slowed conduction across atrial muscle, which accounts for most of the normal PR interval","AV nodal cells generate slow-response action potentials whose upstroke depends on Ca²⁺ current, whereas His–Purkinje and ventricular myocytes depolarize through fast Na⁺ current","The drug prolonged the ventricular plateau (phase 2), and the PR interval includes part of ventricular repolarization","The drug increased vagal release of acetylcholine onto the AV node"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The PR interval runs from the start of atrial depolarization through AV nodal, His bundle, bundle branch, and Purkinje conduction to the start of ventricular activation. Most of it is AV nodal delay. The AV node has small fibers, slow-response action potentials, and a conduction velocity of about 0.05 m/s. Because nodal phase 0 is carried by Ca²⁺ current, blocking L-type channels slows the nodal upstroke and conduction, and the PR lengthens to 240 ms. That is first-degree AV block, and the lecture lists Ca²⁺ channel blockers as a cause of acquired AV block. His–Purkinje fibers (1–4 m/s) and ventricular muscle depolarize through fast INa, so ventricular depolarization (the QRS) is unchanged. The modest sinus slowing reflects the same Ca²⁺ dependence in the SA node.","whyWrong":["Purkinje fibers are fast-response cells with a Na⁺-driven upstroke. Slowed His–Purkinje conduction would also widen the QRS, and the QRS is unchanged.","Atrial muscle conducts quickly (about 1 m/s) using a Na⁺-driven upstroke and contributes only a small part of the PR. The bulk of the PR interval is AV nodal delay.","","The PR interval ends at the onset of the QRS and contains no ventricular repolarization. A longer ventricular plateau would lengthen the QT.","Verapamil blocks nodal Ca²⁺ channels directly; it does not act by increasing acetylcholine release."],"objective":"Relate the Ca²⁺-dependent slow-response AP of the AV node to its slow conduction and to PR prolongation by L-type Ca²⁺ channel blockade, with sparing of the QRS.","source":"Electrophysiology I–III (Beaser): AV node, conduction velocities, PR interval","ecg":{"layout":"strip","leads":["II"],"rate":64,"pr":240}},{"id":"ecg-04","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Rate & rhythm","difficulty":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man has a pre-employment ECG recorded at standard paper speed. His lead II rhythm strip is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best describes his rhythm?","figure":null,"choices":["Normal sinus rhythm at about 100/min","Normal sinus rhythm at about 75/min","Normal sinus rhythm at about 88/min","Sinus tachycardia at about 115/min","Ectopic atrial rhythm at about 88/min"],"answer":2,"explanation":"3 large boxes plus 2 small boxes is 17 small boxes. At 40 ms per small box, the R–R interval is 680 ms. Rate = 60 / 0.68 s ≈ 88/min, which is the same as 1500 / 17 small boxes. The quick 300-rule check agrees: 3 large boxes would be 100/min and 4 would be 75/min, so a little more than 3 boxes gives a rate between those values. Upright P waves in lead II with 1:1 P:QRS and a normal PR indicate sinus origin, and 60–100/min is the normal range. The rhythm is normal sinus rhythm.","whyWrong":["100/min is 300/3. It ignores the extra 2 small boxes, which lengthen the R–R to 680 ms.","75/min would need an R–R of 4 large boxes (800 ms). The interval here is shorter than that.","","Sinus tachycardia means a rate above 100/min. A rate of 115/min would need an R–R of about 520 ms (13 small boxes).","An ectopic atrial focus low in the atria gives inverted P waves in lead II. These P waves are upright, which fits SA nodal origin."],"objective":"Calculate heart rate from the R–R interval using box counts (1500/small boxes or 300/large boxes) and identify sinus rhythm.","source":"Electrophysiology III (Beaser): rate measurement and rhythm analysis","ecg":{"layout":"strip","leads":["II"],"rate":88.235,"pr":160,"start":300}},{"id":"ecg-05","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Rate & rhythm","difficulty":1,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman has had 2 days of intermittent palpitations. Her radial pulse is irregular. Her 10-second rhythm strip (leads II and V1) is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the rhythm and ventricular rate?","figure":null,"choices":["Atrial fibrillation with a ventricular rate of about 140/min","Atrial flutter with variable conduction and a ventricular rate of about 84/min","Multifocal atrial tachycardia with a ventricular rate of about 84/min","Atrial fibrillation with a ventricular rate of about 84/min","Sinus arrhythmia at about 84/min"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An irregularly irregular rhythm, no discrete P waves, and a chaotic fibrillatory baseline together indicate atrial fibrillation. Its mechanism is chaotic micro-reentry in the atria, often triggered around the pulmonary veins in the left atrium. When the R–R interval keeps changing, the box methods are unreliable, so use the course's 10-second strip method: number of QRS complexes × 6 = 14 × 6 ≈ 84/min. The narrow QRS shows that the impulses the AV node lets through travel down the normal His–Purkinje system.","whyWrong":["Multiplying by 10 is the error here. A standard rhythm strip is 10 seconds long, so the count is multiplied by 6 to get beats per minute.","Atrial flutter gives regular, uniform sawtooth flutter waves, best seen in the inferior leads. This baseline is chaotic and has no organized atrial waves.","Multifocal atrial tachycardia has distinct P waves of several different shapes before the QRS complexes. This strip has no discrete P waves.","","Sinus arrhythmia has a normal upright P wave before every QRS, with gradual variation in rate. Here there are no P waves."],"objective":"Recognize atrial fibrillation and estimate the ventricular rate of an irregular rhythm with the 10-second-strip method.","source":"Electrophysiology III (Beaser): rate methods; atrial fibrillation","ecg":{"layout":"strip","leads":["II","V1"],"rhythm":"af","count":14,"fAmp":0.075,"seed":3,"fDir":[-0.2,0.45,0.9]}},{"id":"ecg-06","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Rate & rhythm","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with long-standing hypertension has had palpitations for 1 day. His pulse is regular at 150/min. His ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best explains this tracing?","figure":null,"choices":["Increased automaticity of the SA node producing sinus tachycardia at 150/min","Multiple chaotic atrial wavelets, often triggered from the pulmonary veins, with irregular AV nodal conduction","A reentrant circuit in the right atrium, typically around the tricuspid annulus, with the AV node conducting every second atrial wave","A reentrant circuit within the ventricular myocardium","An accessory pathway that bypasses the AV node and conducts every atrial impulse"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Atrial waves every 200 ms (one large box) give an atrial rate of 300/min. QRS complexes every 400 ms (two large boxes) give a ventricular rate of 150/min, so conduction is 2:1. Regular sawtooth atrial activity, best seen in the inferior leads, is atrial flutter: a reentrant circuit in the atrium, usually around the tricuspid annulus. The AV node conducts slowly and has a long refractory period, so it cannot follow 300 impulses per minute and passes every other one, giving a regular rate of 150. A regular narrow-complex tachycardia at almost exactly 150/min should raise suspicion of flutter with 2:1 block.","whyWrong":["Sinus tachycardia has one upright P wave per QRS with a flat baseline between them. Here there are two atrial waves per QRS, in a continuous sawtooth pattern at 300/min.","This describes atrial fibrillation, which has no organized atrial waves and an irregularly irregular ventricular response. This rhythm is regular, with uniform atrial waves.","","Ventricular reentry (ventricular tachycardia) causes a regular wide-complex tachycardia that is not tied to atrial waves. These QRS complexes are narrow and follow every second atrial wave in a fixed pattern.","If an accessory pathway conducted every atrial impulse, the ventricular rate would be about 300/min and the QRS complexes would be pre-excited (wide). The fixed 2:1 ratio reflects AV nodal filtering."],"objective":"Recognize atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction and explain how the AV node's slow conduction and refractoriness set the ventricular rate.","source":"Electrophysiology III (Beaser): reentrant atrial rhythms (atrial flutter)","ecg":{"rhythm":"flutter","atrialRate":300,"ratio":2,"fAmp":0.3,"qrs":{"tamp":0.18}}},{"id":"ecg-07","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Axis","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a routine ECG. He has no symptoms, and an echocardiogram is normal. His ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely interpretation?","figure":null,"choices":["Normal axis; a net-negative lead II is a normal physiologic leftward variant","Right axis deviation, consistent with left posterior fascicular block","Right axis deviation, consistent with right ventricular hypertrophy","Left axis deviation (more negative than −30°), consistent with left anterior fascicular block","Extreme (northwest) axis, suggesting ventricular tachycardia or lead reversal"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Lead I is positive and aVF is negative, so the axis lies between 0° and −90°. Lead II (+60°) is perpendicular to −30°. A net-negative QRS in lead II therefore puts the axis beyond −30°, which is pathologic left axis deviation. The QRS is narrow-normal, the inferior leads have initial r waves (no Q waves, which argues against an inferior infarct), and voltages are normal (no LVH). The best explanation is left anterior fascicular block. With the anterior fascicle blocked, the LV is activated from the posterior fascicle, and the main depolarization vector points superiorly and leftward. Left posterior fascicular block does the opposite and causes right axis deviation, with lead I negative and aVF positive.","whyWrong":["A physiologic leftward axis (0° to −30°) still has a positive QRS in lead II. When lead II is net negative, the axis is beyond −30°, which is pathologic.","In right axis deviation lead I is negative and aVF is positive. Here lead I is positive and aVF is negative. LPFB produces right axis deviation, so it does not fit.","Right ventricular hypertrophy moves the axis rightward, giving a negative lead I and positive aVF. This tracing shows the opposite pattern.","","An extreme (northwest) axis has a negative QRS in both lead I and aVF. Lead I is strongly positive here."],"objective":"Use leads I, II, and aVF to identify pathologic left axis deviation and attribute it to left anterior (rather than posterior) fascicular block.","source":"Electrophysiology II (Beaser): mean electrical axis and its causes; Small Group 3 axis question","ecg":{"rate":70,"pr":170,"qrs":{"type":"lafb"}}},{"id":"ecg-08","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Axis","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has a preoperative ECG, shown below.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the mean QRS axis?","figure":null,"choices":["Pathologic left axis deviation (beyond −30°)","Normal axis, between 0° and −30°","Right axis deviation (beyond +90°)","Normal axis, between +60° and +90°","Extreme axis deviation (between −90° and ±180°)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A positive lead I places the axis between −90° and +90°. A slightly negative aVF (+90°) places it slightly negative to 0°, so it lies between 0° and −90° but close to 0°. A positive lead II (+60°) means the axis is within 90° of +60°, so it is more positive than −30°. Together these put the axis between 0° and −30°, a physiologic leftward axis, which the course counts as normal (normal range −30° to +90°). The small-group shortcut gives the same answer: if the QRS is upright in both I and II, the axis is normal. Pathologic left axis deviation requires lead II to be net negative.","whyWrong":["For the axis to be beyond −30°, lead II (+60°, which is perpendicular to −30°) would have to be net negative. Here lead II is clearly positive.","","Right axis deviation needs a negative lead I and a positive aVF. Lead I is strongly positive here.","An axis between +60° and +90° would give a clearly positive aVF and lead III. Here aVF is slightly negative and lead III is negative.","Extreme axis deviation needs both lead I and aVF to be negative. Lead I is positive."],"objective":"Recognize that a physiologic leftward axis (0° to −30°, positive I and II with negative aVF) falls within the normal range.","source":"Electrophysiology II (Beaser): QRS mean electrical axis; Small Group 3","ecg":{"rate":68,"pr":150,"qrs":{"axis":-15}}},{"id":"ecg-09","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Intervals & blocks","difficulty":1,"stem":"A 21-year-old collegiate swimmer wears an ambulatory monitor after a single episode of lightheadedness. The lead II strip recorded during sleep is shown. While she is awake and exercising, every P wave conducts with a PR of 160 ms.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","figure":null,"choices":["First-degree AV block","Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type II, in the His–Purkinje system","Third-degree (complete) AV block","Sinus pause from SA nodal arrest","Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type I (Wenckebach), most likely within the AV node"],"answer":4,"explanation":"The PR interval lengthens progressively until one P wave is not conducted, then the cycle resets. That is second-degree AV block, Mobitz type I (Wenckebach). Several features point to the AV node, under strong vagal influence: the QRS is narrow, the block happens only during sleep in a trained athlete, and it disappears with exercise, when vagal tone falls and sympathetic tone rises. As the lecture notes, Mobitz I is usually benign, and pacing is not indicated unless symptoms are significant. Mobitz II instead has a constant PR with a sudden non-conducted P wave, comes from disease below the AV node, and usually requires pacing.","whyWrong":["In first-degree AV block the PR is longer than 200 ms but every P wave is conducted. Here a P wave is dropped after the PR progressively lengthens.","In Mobitz II the PR is constant before a dropped beat. Here the PR lengthens progressively, and the narrow QRS and vagal pattern point to the AV node.","In third-degree block the P waves and QRS complexes are completely dissociated. Here most P waves conduct, with a PR that changes in a predictable pattern.","In a sinus pause the P wave itself is missing. Here the P waves stay regular; one simply fails to conduct.",""],"objective":"Distinguish Mobitz I from Mobitz II and other AV blocks by the PR-interval pattern, and recognize the benign, vagally mediated AV nodal form.","source":"Electrophysiology III (Beaser): conduction analysis, 2nd-degree AV block","ecg":{"layout":"strip","leads":["II"],"rate":60,"conduction":"mobitz1","prSeq":[200,260,300],"start":200}},{"id":"ecg-10","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Intervals & blocks","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man is brought in after two fainting episodes. Pulse is 34/min and regular; BP is 102/60. His lead II rhythm strip is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best describes this rhythm?","figure":null,"choices":["Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type II, with 2:1 conduction","Third-degree AV block with an AV junctional escape rhythm","Sinus bradycardia with right bundle branch block","Third-degree AV block with a ventricular escape rhythm","Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type I"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The atrial rate is 60 / 0.68 s ≈ 88/min and the ventricular rate is 60 / 1.76 s ≈ 34/min. The PR interval varies randomly, so the P waves and QRS complexes are independent, with the atria beating faster. This is complete (third-degree) heart block. The escape site is shown by the QRS width and rate. A wide QRS means the ventricles are being activated cell to cell rather than through the His–Purkinje system, and a rate of 30–40/min is typical of a ventricular (Purkinje) escape pacemaker. The lecture names the Purkinje fibers as the main site of escape pacemakers and describes complete heart block escapes as usually wide at 30–40 bpm, with permanent pacing indicated.","whyWrong":["In 2:1 Mobitz II, every other P wave conducts with a constant PR, so the ventricular rate would be exactly half the atrial rate (about 44/min). Here the PR varies randomly and the ratio is not fixed.","A junctional escape travels down the His–Purkinje system, so it produces a narrow QRS at about 40–60/min. A 160-ms QRS at 34/min points to a ventricular escape.","The atrial (P) rate is 88/min, so this is not sinus bradycardia. The P waves are also not linked to the QRS complexes.","","In Mobitz I most P waves conduct, and the PR lengthens progressively before a dropped beat. Here no P wave has a consistent relationship to a QRS."],"objective":"Recognize third-degree AV block from AV dissociation and use QRS width and escape rate to locate the escape pacemaker.","source":"Electrophysiology II & III (Beaser): escape pacemakers, 3rd-degree AV block","ecg":{"layout":"strip","leads":["II"],"rhythm":"chb","atrialRate":88.235,"rate":34.09,"pStart":150,"vStart":700}},{"id":"ecg-11","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Intervals & blocks","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman has an ECG before knee surgery. She has no symptoms. Her ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best explains this QRS morphology?","figure":null,"choices":["Block in the right bundle branch, so the left ventricle is activated normally and the right ventricle is activated last by slow cell-to-cell spread","Block in the left bundle branch, so the septum is activated from right to left and the left ventricle is activated last","An accessory pathway that pre-excites the ventricles by bypassing the AV node","Block in the left anterior fascicle, delaying activation of the anterosuperior left ventricle","A ventricular ectopic focus firing on every beat"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A wide QRS (140 ms) with a sinus P wave and normal, constant PR before every beat indicates delayed conduction within the ventricles. The septum still activates left to right, producing the small r in V1 and the q in V6. The LV then depolarizes normally through the left bundle, producing the S in V1 and the R in V6. Without a working right bundle, the RV is activated last, by slow myocyte-to-myocyte spread. That late, slow, rightward and anterior force produces the terminal R′ in V1 and the broad slurred S in I and V6. This is right bundle branch block. In left bundle branch block the initial septal vector is lost (no q in V6), and the QRS is a broad, monophasic R in I and V6 with a deep QS or rS in V1.","whyWrong":["","LBBB removes the normal septal q wave in V6 and produces broad, notched monophasic R waves in I and V6 with a deep, predominantly negative V1. It does not cause a terminal R′ in V1.","Pre-excitation through an accessory pathway shortens the PR to less than 120 ms and adds a delta wave. Here the PR is a normal 170 ms.","Left anterior fascicular block causes left axis deviation with only minimal QRS widening. Here the axis is normal and the QRS has a classic RBBB shape.","A ventricular ectopic beat has no related P wave before it. Here every QRS follows a sinus P wave with a constant PR."],"objective":"Recognize RBBB morphology (rSR′ in V1, broad slurred S in I/V6), explain it by the late, slow activation of the RV, and contrast it with LBBB.","source":"Electrophysiology II & III (Beaser): ventricular depolarization sequence, QRS duration; ECG practice reads","ecg":{"rate":74,"pr":170,"qrs":{"type":"rbbb"}}},{"id":"ecg-12","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Intervals & blocks","difficulty":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman faints while standing in line and regains consciousness within seconds. Three days ago she started a medication known to block the rapid delayed-rectifier K⁺ current (IKr, encoded by HERG). Electrolytes are normal. Her ECG is shown; the R–R interval measures 810 ms and the QT interval measures 450 ms.\n\nWhich of the following statements is most accurate?","figure":null,"choices":["QTc ≈ 405 ms, which is normal, so the drug has not meaningfully affected ventricular repolarization","QTc ≈ 500 ms, caused by slowed phase 0 Na⁺ entry in ventricular myocytes","QTc ≈ 555 ms, caused by prolonged conduction through the AV node","QTc ≈ 500 ms, caused by increased IK1 that lengthens the plateau","QTc ≈ 500 ms, caused by slowed phase 3 repolarization of ventricular myocytes, which predisposes to torsades de pointes"],"answer":4,"explanation":"QTc = QT / √RR (RR in seconds) = 450 / √0.81 = 450 / 0.9 = 500 ms. That is above the normal limit of 460 ms, so the QT is prolonged. The heart rate is 60 / 0.81 ≈ 74/min. The QT interval spans ventricular depolarization plus repolarization. IKr is one of the delayed-rectifier K⁺ currents that repolarize the cell in phase 3; the lecture lists HERG, KvLQT1, and KCNQ1 polymorphisms here. Blocking IKr slows phase 3 and prolongs the ventricular action potential, which lengthens the QT. A long QTc predisposes to torsades de pointes, a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia that can cause syncope or cardiac arrest.","whyWrong":["This multiplies QT by √RR (450 × 0.9 ≈ 405). The correction divides by √RR, which gives 500 ms, a prolonged value.","Phase 0 Na⁺ entry governs depolarization and conduction speed, and slowing it would widen the QRS, which is normal (90 ms). The drug blocks a K⁺ current involved in repolarization.","555 ms comes from dividing by RR instead of √RR. AV nodal conduction is part of the PR interval (normal at 160 ms), not the QT.","IK1 is the inward rectifier that holds the resting potential and helps finish repolarization. More IK1 would shorten the action potential, and the drug blocks IKr, not IK1.",""],"objective":"Calculate QTc with Bazett's correction and link IKr (HERG) blockade to delayed phase 3 repolarization, QT prolongation, and torsades risk.","source":"Electrophysiology I (Beaser): phase 3 delayed rectifiers; Electrophysiology III: QT interval and torsades","ecg":{"rate":74.07,"pr":160,"qt":450}},{"id":"ecg-13","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Ectopy & arrhythmia","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man notices occasional “skipped beats” after drinking several cups of coffee. His lead II rhythm strip is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best explains why the early beat is wide and why a pause follows it?","figure":null,"choices":["An atrial ectopic focus that resets the SA node; the wide QRS reflects aberrant conduction and the pause is less than compensatory","An AV junctional focus whose impulse travels down the His–Purkinje system and retrogradely into the atria","A ventricular focus whose impulse spreads cell to cell outside the His–Purkinje system; the SA node is not reset, so a full compensatory pause follows","An intermittent right bundle branch block affecting a single sinus beat","A premature discharge of the SA node that is conducted normally to the ventricles"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An early beat that is wide and bizarre, has no P wave before it, and has a T wave pointing the opposite way is a premature ventricular contraction (PVC). Normal beats are narrow because the His–Purkinje system (about 4 m/s) activates both ventricles almost at once. A ventricular focus instead spreads by slow myocyte-to-myocyte conduction, so its QRS is wide. Its ST–T points the other way because repolarization follows the abnormal depolarization sequence. The SA node usually keeps firing on schedule. The next sinus P wave arrives while the conduction system and ventricles are still refractory from the PVC, so it is not conducted, and the following sinus beat comes on time. The two R waves around the PVC are therefore exactly 2 × 800 = 1,600 ms apart, a full compensatory pause. A PAC usually resets the SA node, which gives a pause shorter than this.","whyWrong":["A PAC has an early, abnormally shaped P wave before it and usually resets the SA node, so the R waves around it are less than 1,600 ms apart. Here there is no P wave and the pause is fully compensatory.","A junctional impulse reaches the ventricles through the His–Purkinje system, so its QRS is narrow and looks like the sinus QRS. A retrograde, inverted P wave often appears near it.","","A single beat with intermittent bundle branch block would arrive on schedule, after a sinus P wave with a normal PR. This beat is early and has no P wave.","An early SA nodal discharge would produce an upright sinus P wave followed by a narrow, normal QRS."],"objective":"Identify a PVC from its timing, missing P wave, wide QRS, discordant T, and full compensatory pause, and explain why it is wide.","source":"Electrophysiology I & III (Beaser): refractory periods, abnormal automaticity, why PVCs are wide; Retired item Q11","ecg":{"layout":"strip","leads":["II"],"rate":75,"pr":160,"ectopy":[{"beat":2,"type":"pvc","coupling":480}]}},{"id":"ecg-14","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Ectopy & arrhythmia","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has an ECG at a sports physical. He has no symptoms and a regular pulse of 50/min. His ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely site of impulse formation?","figure":null,"choices":["Sinoatrial node, near the junction of the superior vena cava and right atrium","Low right atrium, near the coronary sinus ostium","Purkinje fibers within the ventricular myocardium","Atrioventricular node (AV junction)","Left atrial myocardium near the pulmonary veins"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Several findings point to the AV junction. The narrow, normal QRS shows the ventricles were activated through the His–Purkinje system, so the focus is at or above the His bundle. A P wave inverted in II, III, and aVF means the atria were depolarized from below upward, away from the inferior leads' positive pole on the left leg. Because the P wave follows the QRS, the impulse reached the ventricles (through the His bundle) before the retrograde wave finished activating the atria, which is typical of a junctional focus. The rate of 50/min also matches the AV node's intrinsic rate of 40–60 bpm when there is no sinus input. A low atrial focus would also produce inverted inferior P waves, but they would come before the QRS with a normal PR of at least 120 ms, because that impulse still has to pass through the AV node.","whyWrong":["A sinus impulse spreads down and to the left, producing an upright P wave in lead II before each QRS.","A low right atrial focus does produce inverted P waves in II, III, and aVF, but they come before the QRS with a normal PR. That impulse still passes through the AV node with its usual delay.","A ventricular or Purkinje focus spreads outside the His–Purkinje system and produces a wide QRS, usually at 20–40/min. This QRS is 80 ms.","","A left atrial focus would make the P wave abnormal mainly in the lateral leads (I, aVL) and would still come before the QRS with a normal PR. It would not appear after the QRS."],"objective":"Use P-wave polarity and its timing relative to the QRS (before with a normal PR vs. after) to tell a junctional rhythm from a low atrial focus.","source":"Electrophysiology II & III (Beaser): AV node intrinsic rate, ectopic atrial focus; Retired item Q12","ecg":{"rhythm":"junctional","rate":50,"retroDelay":110}},{"id":"ecg-15","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Ischemia & full ECG reads","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with diabetes has had 1 hour of crushing chest pressure. BP 128/80, HR 96. Her ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely cause of these findings?","figure":null,"choices":["Acute occlusion of the right coronary artery","Acute occlusion of the left anterior descending artery","A completed (old) infarction, with no ongoing ischemia","Acute occlusion of the left circumflex artery","Acute occlusion of the left main coronary artery"],"answer":3,"explanation":"ST elevation in the contiguous lateral leads (I, aVL, V5, V6, extending into V4) indicates acute transmural injury of the lateral LV wall, which is usually supplied by the left circumflex artery. The ST depression in III and aVF is reciprocal, and the ST depression in V1–V2 is the mirror image of injury on the back (posterolateral) wall, which the circumflex also commonly supplies. The course groups leads by territory: inferior (II, III, aVF) usually maps to the right coronary artery, septal/anterior (V1–V4) to the LAD, and lateral (I, aVL, V5–V6) to the circumflex. On timing, ST elevation signals acute injury, T-wave inversion signals ischemia or an evolving infarct, and pathologic Q waves signal established necrosis.","whyWrong":["An RCA occlusion usually causes ST elevation in the inferior leads (II, III, aVF). Here those leads show ST depression.","An LAD occlusion usually causes ST elevation across the septal and anterior leads (V1–V4). Here V1–V2 show ST depression, not elevation, and the elevation is centered on I, aVL, V5 and V6.","Ongoing ST elevation with chest pain that began 1 hour ago and no Q waves indicates acute injury, not an old infarction. A completed infarct would show Q waves, usually without persistent ST elevation.","","A left main occlusion would threaten the whole LAD and circumflex territory, with widespread findings (the course labels aVR as the left main lead). Here the elevation is confined to the lateral leads and there is no anterior (V1–V3) ST elevation."],"objective":"Localize acute ST-elevation injury to a coronary territory using contiguous lead groups, and recognize reciprocal ST depression.","source":"Electrophysiology II (Beaser): anatomically contiguous leads; Electrophysiology III: ST segment","ecg":{"rate":96,"pr":150,"st":{"mm":3.5,"dir":[1,-0.7,-0.2]}}},{"id":"ecg-16","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Ischemia & full ECG reads","difficulty":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man comes for follow-up. Six weeks ago he had 4 hours of severe chest pressure with nausea but did not seek care. He has no symptoms today. His ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following is the most accurate interpretation?","figure":null,"choices":["Sinus bradycardia with first-degree AV block, left axis deviation, and an acute inferior ST-elevation MI requiring emergent reperfusion","Normal sinus rhythm at 60/min with first-degree AV block and a prior inferior infarction","Sinus bradycardia with first-degree AV block, left axis deviation, and inferior Q waves with T-wave inversion consistent with a completed (prior) inferior infarction","Sinus bradycardia with Mobitz type I second-degree AV block and a prior anterior infarction","Sinus bradycardia with first-degree AV block and left anterior fascicular block, with no evidence of infarction"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Work through the read in order. Rate: 300 / 6 large boxes = 50/min, with upright P waves in lead II and 1:1 conduction, so sinus bradycardia. PR: 7 × 40 = 280 ms and constant, so first-degree AV block. QRS: 80 ms, normal. QTc: 440 / √1.2 ≈ 402 ms, normal. Axis: lead I positive with II and aVF negative, so left axis deviation; the lecture lists inferior MI as a cause. Pathologic Q waves (deep, at least 40 ms) in the contiguous inferior leads, with T-wave inversion and no ST elevation, indicate a completed inferior infarction from weeks ago. This territory is usually the RCA, which in most people also supplies the AV node, so the accompanying bradycardia and PR prolongation fit. LAFB also causes left axis deviation, but its inferior complexes start with a small r wave (rS), not a Q wave.","whyWrong":["The ST segments are isoelectric, so there is no acute ST-elevation injury. Q waves with inverted T waves six weeks after the event show a completed infarct.","6 large boxes between R waves gives 300 / 6 = 50/min, which is sinus bradycardia, not 60/min.","","The PR is constant at 280 ms and every P wave is conducted, so this is first-degree block, not Mobitz I. The Q waves are in the inferior leads, and the precordial leads are normal, so the infarct is inferior, not anterior.","LAFB produces rS complexes, with an initial r wave, in II, III, and aVF. Deep, broad initial Q waves there indicate inferior infarction, which is also what shifts the axis leftward."],"objective":"Perform a full systematic ECG read (rate, rhythm, intervals, axis, morphology) and recognize a completed inferior infarction from pathologic Q waves.","source":"Electrophysiology III (Beaser): systematic ECG approach; ECG practice reads; Small Group 3 (inferior MI case)","ecg":{"rate":50,"pr":280,"qt":440,"qrs":{"axis":-35,"q":{"amp":0.85,"dir":[0.1,-1,0],"w":14},"tdir":[0.6,-0.8,0.45],"tamp":0.3}}},{"id":"ecg-17","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","subtopic":"Ischemia & full ECG reads","difficulty":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has had 1 week of worsening shortness of breath that began after a viral illness. BP 94/78, HR 120. His jugular veins are distended and his heart sounds are muffled. His ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following is the most accurate interpretation?","figure":null,"choices":["Normal sinus rhythm at 100/min with low QRS voltage","Atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction and low QRS voltage","Sinus tachycardia with ventricular bigeminy (every other beat is a PVC)","Sinus tachycardia with an acute anterior ST-elevation MI","Sinus tachycardia with low QRS voltage and electrical alternans, consistent with a large pericardial effusion"],"answer":4,"explanation":"The rate is 300 / 2.5 = 120/min (or 60 / 0.5 s). With upright P waves and 1:1 conduction, the rhythm is sinus tachycardia. The lecture explains that surface ECG amplitude depends on tissue impedance and on vector direction relative to each lead. Fluid around the heart weakens the signal reaching the skin, producing low voltage. Electrical alternans happens because the heart swings within the effusion, so the orientation of its electrical vector to the leads changes from beat to beat and QRS amplitude alternates. Together with hypotension, a narrow pulse pressure, jugular venous distension, and muffled heart sounds (Beck's triad), this points to tamponade. The tachycardia is the baroreflex response to falling stroke volume.","whyWrong":["R–R of 2.5 large boxes gives 300 / 2.5 = 120/min, which is sinus tachycardia, not 100/min. This option also leaves out the beat-to-beat alternation.","Flutter produces sawtooth atrial waves at about 300/min, two per QRS with 2:1 conduction. Here there is one upright P wave per QRS with a constant PR.","PVCs are early and wide with no P wave before them. Here every beat is regular, 80 ms wide, and preceded by a sinus P wave; only the height changes.","There is no ST elevation. The abnormality is low, alternating QRS voltage, not injury current.",""],"objective":"Recognize low voltage and electrical alternans as ECG signs of a large pericardial effusion and explain them using the determinants of ECG amplitude.","source":"Small Group 2 (cardiac tamponade case); Electrophysiology II (Beaser): determinants of ECG amplitude","ecg":{"rate":120,"pr":140,"lowVoltage":0.38,"alternans":0.5}},{"id":"ecg-18","subtopic":"Intervals & blocks","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has an ECG before cataract surgery. She has no chest pain and her troponin is normal. Her ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best explains the QRS morphology?","choices":["Block in the left bundle branch, so the septum depolarizes right-to-left and the LV is activated late by slow cell-to-cell spread","Block in the right bundle branch, so the RV is activated last by slow cell-to-cell spread","Block in the left anterior fascicle, so the LV is activated through the posterior fascicle","An acute anteroseptal infarction producing QS complexes in V1–V3","Ventricular tachycardia arising from the right ventricular outflow tract"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Every QRS follows a sinus P wave with a constant PR, but the QRS is wide (about 150 ms, almost 4 small boxes), so ventricular activation is delayed. V1 shows a deep, broad, mostly negative complex, while I, aVL, V5 and V6 show broad, notched, monophasic R waves with no septal q wave. That is left bundle branch block. With the left bundle blocked, the septum is depolarized from the right side (right-to-left), which removes the normal small q in the lateral leads and the small r in V1. The LV is then activated last by slow myocyte-to-myocyte spread, producing a broad leftward and posterior force (tall notched R in I/V6, deep S/QS in V1). Repolarization is also abnormal, so ST–T waves point opposite the main QRS deflection (discordant), which is expected and not by itself ischemia.","whyWrong":["","RBBB keeps the normal septal vector (small r in V1, q in V6) and adds a terminal rightward-anterior R′ in V1 with a broad slurred S in I and V6. Here V1 is deeply negative with no R′ and the lateral leads have broad notched R waves.","LAFB causes left axis deviation with only minimal QRS widening (<120 ms) and rS complexes in II, III and aVF. This QRS is ~150 ms with a classic LBBB shape.","QS complexes in V1–V3 can follow septal infarction, but this tracing has broad notched R waves in I/V6 with loss of septal q waves and a wide QRS — the global conduction pattern of LBBB. Her troponin is normal.","VT is a wide-complex tachycardia without a related P wave. This rhythm is about 78/min with a sinus P wave and constant PR before every QRS."],"objective":"Recognize LBBB (wide QRS, broad notched R in I/V6 without septal q, deep QS/rS in V1, discordant ST–T) and explain it from the sequence of ventricular activation.","source":"ECG II–III (Electrophysiology II/III lectures); ECG Reading Room small group","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","figure":null,"ecg":{"rate":78,"pr":170,"qrs":{"type":"lbbb"}}},{"id":"ecg-19","subtopic":"Intervals & blocks","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has had several episodes of sudden lightheadedness. His 12-lead ECG shows right bundle branch block. His lead II rhythm strip is shown.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis and site of block?","choices":["Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type II, in the His–Purkinje system below the AV node","Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type I (Wenckebach), within the AV node","First-degree AV block within the AV node","Third-degree AV block with a junctional escape rhythm","Blocked premature atrial contractions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"P waves march out regularly (constant P–P). The PR interval of the conducted beats is constant (about 180 ms, 4.5 small boxes), and then a P wave suddenly fails to conduct, here every third P wave (3:2 conduction). A dropped beat without progressive PR prolongation is Mobitz type II. Mobitz II usually reflects disease below the AV node, in the His bundle or bundle branches, which fits the wide, bundle-branch-block QRS. Because infranodal block can progress abruptly to complete heart block with a slow, unreliable ventricular escape, it explains his lightheadedness and is an indication for pacing. Mobitz I, by contrast, shows progressive PR lengthening before the dropped beat and is usually located in the AV node.","whyWrong":["","Mobitz I shows progressive PR lengthening until a P wave is dropped, and the PR after the pause is the shortest. Here the PR is identical before every conducted QRS.","First-degree AV block has a PR >200 ms with every P wave conducted. Here some P waves are not followed by a QRS.","In third-degree block P waves and QRS complexes are completely unrelated, with a varying PR. Here every conducted beat has the same PR and the QRS complexes are grouped, not regular.","A blocked PAC is an early P wave (often deforming the T wave) that fails to conduct. These non-conducted P waves arrive exactly on time with the sinus P–P interval."],"objective":"Distinguish Mobitz II (constant PR with sudden dropped beats, infranodal, often with BBB) from Mobitz I and other causes of non-conducted P waves.","source":"Electrophysiology III (AV blocks); ECG Reading Room small group","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","figure":null,"ecg":{"layout":"strip","leads":["II"],"rate":78,"pr":180,"conduction":"mobitz2","ratio":3,"qrs":{"type":"rbbb"}}},{"id":"ecg-20","subtopic":"Ischemia & full ECG reads","difficulty":1,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has had 45 minutes of substernal pressure radiating to his jaw, with nausea and diaphoresis. BP 118/74, HR 62. His ECG is shown.\n\nOcclusion of which artery most likely explains these findings?","choices":["Right coronary artery","Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex artery","Left main coronary artery","No acute occlusion; these are changes of an old, completed infarct"],"answer":0,"explanation":"There is ST-segment elevation in the contiguous inferior leads II, III and aVF (greatest in III), with reciprocal ST depression in aVL (and I). Inferior-wall injury is most often caused by right coronary artery occlusion, since in most people (right-dominant circulation) the RCA gives rise to the posterior descending artery that supplies the inferior LV wall. ST elevation greater in III than in II also favors the RCA over the circumflex. The RCA usually supplies the SA and AV nodes, so sinus bradycardia and AV block are common accompaniments. Lead groupings from the course: inferior (II, III, aVF) → RCA; septal/anterior (V1–V4) → LAD; lateral (I, aVL, V5–V6) → circumflex.","whyWrong":["","An LAD occlusion produces ST elevation in V1–V4. The precordial ST segments here are not elevated.","A circumflex occlusion usually produces lateral ST elevation (I, aVL, V5–V6). Here aVL and I show ST depression, the reciprocal pattern of inferior injury.","Left main occlusion threatens the LAD and circumflex territories, with widespread anterior/lateral changes and ST elevation in aVR. The changes here are inferior.","An old infarct shows pathologic Q waves, often with T inversion, and isoelectric ST segments. Ongoing ST elevation with 45 minutes of pain is acute injury."],"objective":"Localize an inferior STEMI (ST elevation II, III, aVF with reciprocal aVL depression) to the right coronary artery.","source":"Electrophysiology III (ischemia/infarct); Small Group 3 (inferior STEMI)","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","figure":null,"ecg":{"rate":62,"pr":170,"st":{"mm":4,"dir":[-0.25,1,0]}}},{"id":"ecg-21","subtopic":"Ischemia & full ECG reads","difficulty":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman who smokes has had 1 hour of crushing chest pain. BP 132/86, HR 92. Her ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely culprit vessel and region of injury?","choices":["Left anterior descending artery; anterior and septal LV wall","Right coronary artery; inferior LV wall","Left circumflex artery; lateral LV wall","Right coronary artery; right ventricular free wall","Posterior descending artery; posterior LV wall"],"answer":0,"explanation":"There is marked ST elevation across the precordial leads V1–V4, extending into V5, with relatively normal limb leads. V1–V2 face the septum and V3–V4 the anterior wall, both supplied by the left anterior descending artery and its septal and diagonal branches. Acute ST elevation in these contiguous leads during ongoing pain indicates transmural injury (STEMI) in the LAD territory. A large anterior infarct removes a big share of contractile LV muscle, so it is the pattern most likely to lower the ESPVR slope and stroke volume.","whyWrong":["","An RCA/inferior infarct produces ST elevation in II, III and aVF. Those leads have no ST elevation here.","A lateral (circumflex) infarct produces ST elevation in I, aVL, V5 and V6, without the dominant V1–V4 elevation seen here.","Right ventricular infarction accompanies an inferior STEMI and is seen in right-sided leads (V4R), with ST elevation in II, III and aVF. This tracing shows anterior injury.","Posterior wall injury produces ST depression (not elevation) in V1–V3, the mirror image of posterior ST elevation."],"objective":"Localize an anterior/anteroseptal STEMI (ST elevation V1–V4) to the LAD.","source":"Electrophysiology III (ischemia/infarct)","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","figure":null,"ecg":{"rate":92,"pr":160,"st":{"mm":3.5,"dir":[0.3,0,1]}}},{"id":"ecg-22","subtopic":"Ectopy & arrhythmia","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman notices an occasional “flip-flop” in her chest. She drinks four energy drinks a day. Her lead II rhythm strip is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best explains the early beat?","choices":["A premature atrial focus whose impulse also resets the SA node, so the pause that follows is less than compensatory","A premature ventricular focus, followed by a full compensatory pause","An AV junctional focus that activates the atria retrogradely","Sinus arrhythmia from respiratory variation in vagal tone","Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type I, with a dropped beat"],"answer":0,"explanation":"One beat arrives early. It is preceded by a P wave that comes too soon and has a different (smaller, flatter) shape from the sinus P waves, and its QRS is narrow and identical to the normal beats. An early, differently shaped P wave with a normal QRS is a premature atrial contraction (PAC): an ectopic atrial focus fires before the SA node, and the impulse then travels through the AV node and His–Purkinje system normally. The ectopic atrial impulse also invades and resets the SA node, so the next sinus beat comes one normal cycle after the PAC. The pause is therefore less than compensatory (the two sinus beats around the PAC are less than two R–R intervals apart), unlike the full compensatory pause after a PVC.","whyWrong":["","A PVC is wide and bizarre with no P wave in front of it, and the sinus node keeps its schedule, giving a fully compensatory pause. This early beat has its own P wave and a narrow QRS.","A junctional beat has no P wave before it or an inverted P wave just before (PR <120 ms) or after the QRS. Here the premature P wave precedes the QRS with a normal PR.","Sinus arrhythmia changes the rate gradually over several beats with identical sinus P waves. Here a single beat is abruptly early and its P wave has a different shape.","In Mobitz I, PR intervals lengthen until a P wave is not conducted; the pause contains a non-conducted P wave. Here the PR is constant and there is no dropped P wave."],"objective":"Recognize a PAC (early, differently shaped P wave; narrow QRS; less-than-compensatory pause) and contrast it with a PVC.","source":"Electrophysiology II–III (ectopy); retired item (abnormal beat origin)","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","figure":null,"ecg":{"layout":"strip","leads":["II"],"rate":60,"pr":160,"ectopy":[{"beat":4,"type":"pac","coupling":640,"pAxis":25,"pr":180}]}},{"id":"ecg-23","subtopic":"Ectopy & arrhythmia","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has a screening ECG before starting on a college rowing team. He has no symptoms and a regular pulse of 66/min. His ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely site of impulse formation?","choices":["Sinoatrial node, at the junction of the superior vena cava and right atrium","Low right atrium, near the coronary sinus ostium","AV node (AV junction)","Bundle of His","Left ventricular free wall"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each QRS is preceded by a P wave, but the P waves are inverted in II, III and aVF, so the atria are being depolarized from below upward (away from the inferior leads' positive pole on the left leg). The PR interval is normal (160 ms, 4 small boxes), which means atrial depolarization came first and the impulse then crossed the AV node with its normal delay. The QRS is narrow, so the ventricles are activated through the His–Purkinje system. Inverted inferior P waves with a normal PR place the focus low in the atria, typically near the coronary sinus ostium (an ectopic or low atrial rhythm).","whyWrong":["A sinus impulse spreads downward and leftward from the high right atrium, producing upright P waves in II, III and aVF. These P waves are inverted.","","A junctional focus activates the atria and ventricles nearly simultaneously, so its retrograde P wave is hidden in the QRS, follows it, or comes less than 120 ms before it. This PR is a normal 160 ms.","A His bundle focus lies below the AV node and would activate the ventricles before or about when the atria depolarize retrogradely; it could not produce a normal 160 ms PR.","A ventricular focus spreads cell to cell outside the His–Purkinje system, giving a wide QRS with no related P wave before it. This QRS is narrow."],"objective":"Use P-wave polarity in the inferior leads together with the PR interval to locate the site of impulse formation.","source":"Retired CV item (low atrial focus); Electrophysiology II","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","figure":null,"ecg":{"rate":66,"pr":160,"pAxis":-70,"pScale":1.5}},{"id":"ecg-24","subtopic":"Axis","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a routine ECG. He has no lung disease, and an echocardiogram shows normal right ventricular size and wall thickness. His ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the mean QRS axis and its most likely cause?","choices":["Right axis deviation, consistent with left posterior fascicular block","Left axis deviation, consistent with left anterior fascicular block","Normal axis of about +60°","Extreme (northwest) axis","Right axis deviation caused by right bundle branch block"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Lead I is net negative (small r, deeper S) and aVF is strongly positive, which places the axis between +90° and +180°: right axis deviation. aVL is the most negative limb lead and III among the most positive, putting the axis around +110° to +120°. The QRS is narrow and there is no RV hypertrophy, lung disease or RBBB pattern, so the most likely cause is left posterior fascicular block: with the posterior fascicle blocked, the LV is activated through the anterior fascicle and the main force swings inferiorly and rightward (small r in I/aVL, small q then tall R in II/III/aVF).","whyWrong":["","LAFB produces left axis deviation: lead I positive with II, III and aVF net negative (rS). Here lead I is negative and the inferior leads are strongly positive.","At +60°, lead I and lead II would both be clearly positive. Here lead I is net negative.","An extreme axis has both lead I and aVF negative. Here aVF is strongly positive.","RBBB produces a wide QRS (≥120 ms) with an rSR′ in V1 and a broad S in I and V6. This QRS is narrow with no R′."],"objective":"Identify right axis deviation from leads I and aVF and attribute it to LPFB when RVH and RBBB are absent.","source":"Electrophysiology II (axis, fascicular blocks); ECG practice reads","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","figure":null,"ecg":{"rate":76,"pr":160,"qrs":{"type":"lpfb"}}},{"id":"ecg-25","subtopic":"Ischemia & full ECG reads","difficulty":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman who recently started an antiemetic comes for a routine visit. She has no chest pain. Her ECG is shown.\n\nWhich interpretation is most accurate?","choices":["Sinus bradycardia with long QT and anterolateral T wave inversions","Normal sinus rhythm with long QT and anterolateral T wave inversions","Sinus bradycardia with anterolateral T wave inversions","Sinus bradycardia with long QT","Normal sinus rhythm with anterolateral ST elevations"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rate: R–R is about 5.5 large boxes, so 300/5.5 ≈ 55/min; upright P waves in II with 1:1 conduction make it sinus bradycardia. Axis is normal (I and aVF positive) and the PR and QRS are normal. QT: from QRS onset to the end of the T wave is about 13–14 small boxes ≈ 540 ms; QTc = 540/√1.1 ≈ 515 ms, clearly prolonged (normal <460 ms), and certain antiemetics block IKr. ST–T: T waves are inverted in I, aVL and V2–V6, an anterolateral distribution, with isoelectric ST segments and no Q waves. Reading systematically (rate → rhythm → axis → intervals → QRS morphology → ischemia) prevents stopping after the first abnormality.","whyWrong":["","A rate of about 55/min is sinus bradycardia, not normal sinus rhythm (60–100/min).","This leaves out the prolonged QT (~540 ms, QTc ~515 ms).","This leaves out the T wave inversions in I, aVL and V2–V6.","The ST segments are isoelectric; the abnormality is inverted T waves, not ST elevation. The rate is also below 60/min."],"objective":"Perform a complete systematic ECG read (rate, rhythm, axis, intervals, morphology, ischemia) and combine the findings into one interpretation.","source":"ECG of the Day practice reads; Electrophysiology III","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","figure":null,"ecg":{"rate":54,"pr":170,"qt":540,"qrs":{"tdir":[-0.55,0.5,-0.65],"tamp":0.3}}},{"id":"ecg-26","subtopic":"Ischemia & full ECG reads","difficulty":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man who had a “heart attack years ago” comes for a preoperative visit. He feels well. His ECG is shown.\n\nWhich interpretation is most accurate?","choices":["Normal sinus rhythm with LAFB, anteroseptal Q waves, and frequent PVCs","Normal sinus rhythm with LPFB, inferior Q waves, and frequent PACs","Atrial fibrillation with LAFB and anteroseptal Q waves","Normal sinus rhythm with LBBB and frequent PVCs","Normal sinus rhythm with LAFB and acute anterior ST elevations"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rhythm: upright P waves before the narrow beats at about 78/min, so the underlying rhythm is normal sinus. Two beats are early, wide and bizarre with no preceding P wave and discordant T waves, followed by a pause: PVCs. Axis: lead I positive while II, III and aVF are net negative with rS complexes, so left axis deviation beyond −30°, consistent with LAFB. Morphology: V1–V3 show QS complexes (no initial r wave) — anteroseptal Q waves from an old septal/anterior infarction. The ST segments are isoelectric, so there is no acute injury. QRS of the sinus beats is narrow, so this is not LBBB.","whyWrong":["","LPFB gives right axis deviation (I negative, aVF positive). Here lead I is positive and the inferior leads are negative. The Q waves are in V1–V3, not II/III/aVF, and the early beats are wide without P waves (PVCs, not PACs).","There are regular P waves before every narrow beat; the irregularity comes only from the premature wide beats.","The sinus beats have a narrow QRS; only the ectopic beats are wide. LBBB would make every beat wide with broad notched R waves in I and V6.","The ST segments are isoelectric. QS complexes without ST elevation in an asymptomatic patient indicate an old infarct, not acute injury."],"objective":"Integrate axis, Q waves and ectopy into a full ECG interpretation in the style of the course practice reads.","source":"ECG of the Day 9 practice reads","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","figure":null,"ecg":{"rate":78,"pr":160,"qrs":{"type":"lafb","q":{"amp":0.75,"dir":[0.1,0,-1],"w":13}},"ectopy":[{"beat":3,"type":"pvc","coupling":420},{"beat":8,"type":"pvc","coupling":430}]}},{"id":"ecg-27","subtopic":"Ectopy & arrhythmia","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man who had a large anterior myocardial infarction 2 years ago presents with palpitations and lightheadedness. BP 88/56 mmHg. His ECG is shown.\n\nWhich of the following best explains this rhythm?","choices":["A reentrant circuit in ventricular myocardium around scar, producing a regular wide-complex tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia with right bundle branch block","Atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction","Atrial fibrillation with a rapid ventricular response","AV nodal reentry producing a narrow-complex tachycardia"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The rhythm is a regular tachycardia at about 165/min (R–R ≈ 1.8 large boxes) with very wide (~160 ms), bizarre, uniform QRS complexes and no P waves visible before them. In a patient with a prior large infarction, a regular wide-complex tachycardia is ventricular tachycardia until proven otherwise. Scar from the old infarct creates zones of slow conduction and unidirectional block that support reentry. Because activation spreads cell to cell instead of through the His–Purkinje system, the QRS is wide. The short diastole and loss of AV synchrony reduce filling and stroke volume, explaining his hypotension.","whyWrong":["","Sinus tachycardia with RBBB would show a P wave before every QRS and an rSR′ in V1 with a broad terminal S in I and V6. No P waves precede these complexes, and the morphology is not RBBB.","Flutter with 2:1 conduction produces sawtooth flutter waves and usually narrow QRS complexes at about 150/min.","Atrial fibrillation is irregularly irregular. This rhythm is perfectly regular.","AV nodal reentry uses the normal His–Purkinje system, so the QRS is narrow. These complexes are very wide."],"objective":"Recognize ventricular tachycardia as a regular wide-complex tachycardia and explain reentry around infarct scar.","source":"Electrophysiology II–III (arrhythmia mechanisms)","topic":"Electrophysiology & ECG","figure":null,"ecg":{"rhythm":"vt","rate":165,"qrs":{"dir1":[-0.6,-0.7,0.35],"dir2":[-0.7,-0.5,0.5]}}}]);
